The three commands (compile,package and install) correspond to different phases in Maven's build lifecycle:

validate
   ↓
generate-sources
   ↓
compile
   ↓
test
   ↓
package
   ↓
verify
   ↓
install
   ↓
deploy
When you execute a phase, Maven runs all previous phases automatically.
1. mvn compile
Runs up to the compile phase.
What it does
Validates the project.
Generates sources (including your OpenAPI-generated code).
Compiles Java source code.
Produces .class files.
Output:
target/
    classes/
        *.class
It does not:
run tests,
create a JAR,
install anything into your local Maven repository.
Use it when

You're coding and just want to verify that the project compiles.

For your project, this is probably the command you'll use most often:

mvn clean compile
2. mvn package

Runs up to the package phase.

It does everything that compile does, plus:

runs tests (unless skipped),
packages the application.

If you're building a Spring Boot application, you'll typically get:

target/

kash-service.jar

or

kash-service.war

depending on your packaging.

Use it when

You want a deployable artifact (JAR/WAR).

Example:

mvn clean package

or skip tests:

mvn clean package -DskipTests
3. mvn install

Runs up to the install phase.

It does everything that package does, plus:

copies the built artifact into your local Maven repository (~/.m2/repository).

For example:

~/.m2/repository/

com/
   bnppf/
      adm/
          kash/
             1.0.0/
                 kash-1.0.0.jar

This allows other Maven projects on your machine to depend on your artifact.

Use it when
You're developing a shared library.
Another local project needs to use your newly built artifact.
Visual comparison
Command	Compiles	Runs Tests	Creates JAR	Installs to ~/.m2
mvn compile	✅	❌	❌	❌
mvn package	✅	✅	✅	❌
mvn install	✅	✅	✅	✅
